From be68b5c43071aaa28d5813053c5130ff984b7fe1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: halt95 <halt95@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 02:49:51 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 93/97] mamba: hold the partial-tail CoW checkpoint until the
 request is freed

With --prefix-match-unit below the block size, a follow-up turn that
resumes at a partial tail (mid-block) never hashes a block-boundary GDN
state. Under retention interval 0 the only boundary kept is the replay
boundary, which lies below the resume point. The request's own tail is
published on its running block and moved into a CoW block by the next
chunk, and the scheduler releases both copy endpoints once the copy has
run. That block then sat at ref 0 in the free queue for the rest of the
request, ahead of its attention blocks, and concurrent prefills popped
it. The hybrid hit is the shortest group's, so the next turn resumed at
0. Under a long-context multi-turn agent load, 7 of 61 follow-ups at
unit 64 (6 at 256/512/1024) came back with cached_tokens 0 on 80-100K
prompts whose whole attention prefix was still cached. Every one
followed a partial-tail resume, none followed a block-aligned one, and
unit 4096 never missed.

The existing retention (VLLM_E2_MAMBA_RETAIN_CHECKPOINT) only held
hashed blocks retired from the table, so it never covered that copy.
Now, under the same switch:

- The running-request CoW branch holds the moved tail block in
  _retained_checkpoints with a ref of its own; the copy retention pair
  is unchanged. The new-request branch holds the hit source after
  _apply_cow for the same reason: a request that stops before its own
  tail still leaves the state it resumed from.
- _retained_checkpoints is keyed by block_hash_num_tokens instead of
  block index, so a sub-block tail key orders correctly against full
  block states; _retire_state_blocks uses the same key. The newest
  position supersedes and releases the older one, so a request still
  holds at most one block per group.
- When a producer moves its tail hashes into its CoW block, another
  request holding that source (a sibling that resumed at the tail while
  it was the producer's live block) follows the hashes to the copy.
- Admission reserves one extra block per Mamba group for a partial-tail
  hit: the held source and the moved tail coexist at the tail step.

The hold is released on every free path (finish, abort, preemption,
deferred pop_blocks_for_free) by the coordinator, last, after the
request's attention blocks, as before. When the hold is active (switch
on, hash unit below the Mamba block) MambaManager logs one startup line
saying so, with the hash unit and block size.

Tests (CPU): a replay of the miss that fails on v2.5.0 with the
retention switch on (next-turn hit 0 instead of the tail) and passes
here; the structural variant; admission accounting; no-leak checks for
a request freed with its copy still queued, in both release orders; and
the moved-hash follow. The retention suite's one block-index assertion
now reads the token position.
